  "narrative": "Compared to last sale (11-12-26): Yearling cattle were too lightly tested to develop an accurate market trend, however higher undertones were noticed on both steers and heifers. Steer calves all sold generally steady on like quality offerings. Heifer calves under 500 lbs were too lightly tested this week to develop an accurate market trend, however lower undertones were noticed on a lower quality offering and fewer takers for this class of calves. Heifer calves 500 lbs and heavier sold mostly steady to 5.00 higher. Demand for calves was mostly moderate to good for light to moderate offerings. Calves were offered in mostly small to moderate package sizes this week. A smaller offering was seen this week compared to last week. Buyers were forced to piece together loads as a result. Quality this week was not as attractive as last weeks offering. Most calves were cutbacks off summer video sales. Demand was similar for like quality offerings. Many short weaned offerings were seen this week, most of these offerings were very full. CME positions continue to rebound today with strong triple digit gains noticed. The January and later contracts all traded 5.00-6.00 higher today. Market activity was mostly moderate to active. A smaller buying pool noticed this week. Billings livestock commission will not sale cows or bulls again this fall on Thursdays. Cows will be sold on Mondays during the fall run. Supply included: 100% Feeder Cattle (55% Steers, 43% Heifers, 2% Bulls). Feeder cattle supply over 600 lbs was 37%.",
